pair D = (0,4);
draw(A--B--C--D)--cycle;
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
[/asy]
Since $P$ and $Q$ are midpoints of $\overline{AB}$ and $\overline{BC}$, it follows that $AP = 1$ and $PB = 3$. Connecting these points forms two congruent squares, $APQD$ and $ABQP$, with area of $1/4$ square units each, as shown below.
[asy]
/* AMC8 2000 #15 Problem Solution */
unitsize(0.5inch);
pair A = (0,0);
pair B = (4,0);
pair C = (4,4);
pair D = (0,4);
pair P = (0,2);
pair Q = (2,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(A--P--D);
draw(B--P--Q);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,W);
label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("2",(0.5,2),W);
label("3",(2,2),N);
label("2",(1,0.5),S);
label("3",(4,2),E);
[/asy]
As shown above, the two smaller squares contain each of the two non-shaded triangles. Thus, when one subtracts the area of the two non-shaded triangles from the area of the square, there must be half of the square's area remaining, i.e., the situation shown in the third figure below.
[asy]
/* AMC8 2000 #15 Problem Solution */
unitsize(0.5inch);
pair A = (0,0);
pair B = (4,0);
pair C = (4,4);
pair D = (0,4);
pair P = (0,2);
pair Q = (2,4);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle);
draw(A--P--D);
draw(B--P--Q);
label("$A$",A,SW);
label("$B$",B,SE);
label("$C$",C,NE);
label("$D$",D,NW);
label("$P$",P,W);
label("$Q$",Q,E);
label("2",(0.5,2),W);
label("3",(2,2),N);
label("2",(1,0.5),S);
label("3",(4,2),E);
label("Shaded",(1,1),S);
[/asy]
This implies the ratio of shaded area to total area of square $ABCD$ is $\frac{1}{2}$. Therefore, the fraction of the interior of the square that is shaded is $\boxed{\frac{1}{2}}$.
